WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:04.630
Speaker 1: We have the latest inflation numbers,
these numbers have been much anticipated,

00:00:04.630 --> 00:00:09.700
there were rumors flying around that year
over year inflation.

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:15.680
Once March 2022, numbers were added in would
get into the double digits, over 10 percent.

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:18.529
And that didn't happen, and we're going to
talk about expectations.

00:00:18.529 --> 00:00:21.320
We're also going to talk about what the number
means.

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:29.849
But first, we all know if we participate in
the American economy, meaning if you buy groceries,

00:00:29.849 --> 00:00:31.789
go out to eat.

00:00:31.789 --> 00:00:34.550
Own a vehicle, a gas vehicle.

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:39.190
Pay utilities, I mean, everything is more
expensive, my electrical bill is up.

00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:41.109
My grocery bill is up.

00:00:41.109 --> 00:00:43.800
People who have gas vehicles see that gas
is more expensive.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:48.969
We know intuitively because of our experience
in the economy that prices are up and the

00:00:48.969 --> 00:00:52.020
question is how much and in what sectors.

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:55.289
And we now have that information.

00:00:55.289 --> 00:01:04.670
And inflation has indeed increased by the
most since 1981, and the consumer price index

00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:07.909
is up 8.5 percent now.

00:01:07.909 --> 00:01:13.250
There are people confused about what this
means, but the reporting is also confusing

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:16.299
when we talk about an eight point five percent
price increase.

00:01:16.299 --> 00:01:21.780
We don't mean that in March, prices went up
for 8.5 percent.

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:24.690
However, a lot of the reporting makes it seem
that way.

00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:29.140
I mean, look at this Yahoo Finance headline,
which says inflation rises by the most since

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:35.409
1981 as the consumer price index jumps 8.5
percent in March.

00:01:35.409 --> 00:01:42.579
I understand why some people are confused,
but so that you know, this number means that

00:01:42.579 --> 00:01:50.119
in the year ending March 2022, the consumer
price index is up 8.5 percent.

00:01:50.119 --> 00:01:53.969
The previous number was February to February.

00:01:53.969 --> 00:01:58.039
The new number eight point five percent is
March to March.

00:01:58.039 --> 00:02:00.369
Prices didn't go up 8.5 percent in March.

00:02:00.369 --> 00:02:03.329
They went up eight point five percent in the
year ending March.

00:02:03.329 --> 00:02:05.590
Now it's on the one hand.

00:02:05.590 --> 00:02:07.799
Why am I talking about this so much?

00:02:07.799 --> 00:02:11.260
On the other hand, if you don't understand
that you don't have any idea what's going

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:15.519
on and there's people all over Twitter and
all over my feed saying, Can can you believe

00:02:15.519 --> 00:02:16.519
it?

00:02:16.519 --> 00:02:18.760
Prices are up almost 10 percent in one month.

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:19.810
No, they're not.

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:20.810
These are high numbers.

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:25.480
These are the highest inflation numbers since
1981 for a one year period.

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:30.750
But unless we all are on the same page about
what this means, we're really not going to

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:31.760
get anywhere.

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:35.519
And the reporting doesn't doesn't make it
particularly more clear.

00:02:35.519 --> 00:02:43.319
Now you look in more detail at the numbers
and you see that there are a number of different

00:02:43.319 --> 00:02:45.120
areas that that are sort of leading this.

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:48.380
Some of the biggest contributors to the increase
were food.

00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:52.209
We know that we know groceries are up.

00:02:52.209 --> 00:02:54.150
We joke a lot about mango prices.

00:02:54.150 --> 00:03:03.129
But but grocery prices are up, shelter rents
and home property properties, real estate

00:03:03.129 --> 00:03:04.970
and gasoline.

00:03:04.970 --> 00:03:10.900
And the index tracking gas prices surged up
eighteen point three percent month on month

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:15.310
in March, comprising more than half of the
total monthly increase.

00:03:15.310 --> 00:03:23.049
So half of the increase in prices in March
was gasoline alone.

00:03:23.049 --> 00:03:29.160
And in February, gasoline had a 6.6 percent
monthly increase.

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:34.830
So this this is if you want to have a real
conversation about this, you kind of have

00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:39.610
to get away from the politics because there's
a lot of this is just Joe Biden's fault.

00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:42.200
And of course, there's global inflation.

00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:44.750
Gas prices are up everywhere.

00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:49.819
The most important aspects of this, to me,
are kind of twofold.

00:03:49.819 --> 00:03:51.930
One is the reaction.

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:56.080
Why is the stock market up today if these
numbers are so terrible?

00:03:56.080 --> 00:04:00.330
And as of right now as we're filming, the
stock market is up significantly today.

00:04:00.330 --> 00:04:07.220
The answer that we are taught and we can all
judge whether it makes sense or not.

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:13.110
Is that the market had already priced in that
the numbers could be even worse?

00:04:13.110 --> 00:04:15.959
There were rumors inflation could be above
10 percent.

00:04:15.959 --> 00:04:18.730
Now the actual economic forecasts were exactly
this.

00:04:18.730 --> 00:04:19.860
They were eight point four percent.

00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:22.470
We got eight point five percent year over
year.

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:27.600
But because the market now has the certainty
that it's not going to be worse than expected.

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:29.290
The market is doing well.

00:04:29.290 --> 00:04:30.330
Some people just don't care.

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:34.120
OK, but if we want to analyze that, that would
be one thing to think about.

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:38.990
The other aspect of this is there are lots
of people pushing hot takes on social media

00:04:38.990 --> 00:04:44.090
that this isn't the real number, that the
real number is above 10 percent, and that

00:04:44.090 --> 00:04:48.430
the metric of the consumer price index doesn't
accurately take that into account.

00:04:48.430 --> 00:04:51.100
Well, that's an argument that you can have
at any time.

00:04:51.100 --> 00:04:55.100
Very often when unemployment numbers come
out, you will see people come out of the woodwork

00:04:55.100 --> 00:05:00.580
and they'll say, you know, when in the unemployment
system, you stop looking for work, you're

00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:02.470
no longer considered unemployed.

00:05:02.470 --> 00:05:04.570
So the number is not accurate.

00:05:04.570 --> 00:05:08.970
That's true, but that's been a reality in
the unemployment system for 40 years.

00:05:08.970 --> 00:05:12.690
Going all the way back to Reagan is that closer
to 50 years now?

00:05:12.690 --> 00:05:20.040
The point is, you can't cherry pick when you
point out the limitations of a metric to try

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:23.630
to make a point about only the latest numbers.

00:05:23.630 --> 00:05:24.630
That's dishonest.

00:05:24.630 --> 00:05:27.670
This is the way that we've measured these
things for a long time.

00:05:27.670 --> 00:05:30.680
My prediction and my prediction is worth as
little as everybody.

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:35.420
Else's I mean, I'm the first to tell you,
humans are terrible at making economic predictions.

00:05:35.420 --> 00:05:39.104
My prediction and this is just an opinion.

00:05:39.104 --> 00:05:44.270
Is that we're basically at the peak here of
inflation and that we're going to start seeing

00:05:44.270 --> 00:05:50.630
a slow decline in year over year inflation
may maybe starting next month with this month's

00:05:50.630 --> 00:05:54.510
numbers, but more likely in the next two to
three months.

00:05:54.510 --> 00:05:55.640
That's my prediction.

00:05:55.640 --> 00:06:00.590
What might delay it a little bit would be
gas prices in general tend to go up over the

00:06:00.590 --> 00:06:04.970
summer, more people driving, taking road trips,
etc. and if that's the case, it may push it

00:06:04.970 --> 00:06:06.430
off even a little more.

00:06:06.430 --> 00:06:12.710
But my sense is, within the next four months,
we are going to start to see a decline that

00:06:12.710 --> 00:07:25.720
that unless something dramatic, changes should
continue.

